    {
      "page_title": "Indexing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Indexing",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A ==database index== is a sorted lookup structure that lets queries __skip the full table scan__.",
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** a separate sorted B-tree mapping column values to row locations",
            "**Why it exists:** scanning 10M rows is O(n); a B-tree lookup is O(log n), about 3-4 page reads",
            "**Used for:** WHERE filters, JOIN keys, ORDER BY, UNIQUE constraints"
          ],
          "style": "star",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE INDEX idx_users_email",
            "  ON users(email);",
            "SELECT * FROM users",
            "WHERE email='a@x.com';",
            "EXPLAIN ANALYZE ...; -- verify"
          ],
          "note": "Always confirm with EXPLAIN ANALYZE"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "Before index",
                "headers": [
                  "Plan",
                  "Rows read"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "Seq Scan",
                    "10,000,000"
                  ]
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "After index",
                "headers": [
                  "Plan",
                  "Rows read"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "Index Scan",
                    "1"
                  ]
                ]
              }
            ],
            "arrow": true
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Composite Index",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Query on (country, created_at)",
            "Uses index?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "country only",
              "{green:yes}"
            ],
            [
              "country + created_at",
              "{green:yes}"
            ],
            [
              "created_at + country",
              "{green:yes}"
            ],
            [
              "created_at only",
              "**no**"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Leftmost-prefix rule",
          "text": "Index (country, created_at) is read left to right: skip country and it is useless."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "WHERE term order does not matter: the planner reorders predicates to match the index."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Benefits vs Things to Consider",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Selective reads: seconds drop to milliseconds",
            "Covering index (INCLUDE cols): answered with no table fetch",
            "Range scans and ORDER BY come free: entries are sorted"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE updates each index: writes slow",
            "Low-cardinality `is_active` boolean rarely benefits",
            "`LOWER(email)` wraps the column and bypasses the index"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Index for your read patterns and order composite columns by the leftmost-prefix rule.\nAlways justify the write-amplification cost of each extra index."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Concept 1 of 10"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "2. Transactions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** statements run as one ==all-or-nothing== unit (BEGIN … COMMIT/ROLLBACK)",
            "**Why it exists:** a crash midway must not leave half-applied state",
            "**Used for:** money transfers, order + inventory updates, multi-row invariants"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal-100",
            "  WHERE id=1;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal+100",
            "  WHERE id=2;",
            "COMMIT;"
          ],
          "note": "2nd UPDATE fails → ROLLBACK restores acct 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "Start",
                "headers": [
                  "id",
                  "bal"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "1",
                    "500"
                  ],
                  [
                    "2",
                    "300"
                  ]
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "After 1st UPDATE",
                "headers": [
                  "id",
                  "bal"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "1",
                    "400"
                  ],
                  [
                    "2",
                    "300"
                  ]
                ],
                "caption": "2nd one fails"
              },
              {
                "title": "After ROLLBACK",
                "headers": [
                  "id",
                  "bal"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "1",
                    "500"
                  ],
                  [
                    "2",
                    "300"
                  ]
                ],
                "caption": "total = 800"
              }
            ],
            "arrow": true
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "BEGIN",
              "opens the unit of work"
            ],
            [
              "COMMIT",
              "makes all changes permanent"
            ],
            [
              "ROLLBACK",
              "undoes everything since BEGIN"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Under the hood: WAL",
          "items": [
            "The ==write-ahead log== records changes before data pages",
            "After a crash, committed work is **redone**",
            "After a crash, uncommitted work is **undone**"
          ],
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Benefits",
          "items": [
            "✓ Invariants hold: total money stays constant",
            "✓ Simple errors: any failure rolls everything back",
            "✓ Others never see your uncommitted state*"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Things to consider",
          "items": [
            "⚠ Long transactions hold locks, bloat log and row versions",
            "⚠ Keep them **short**",
            "⚠ No network calls (payment API, email) while open"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "* Isolation level",
          "text": "How much others see depends on the level. See pages 3 and 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "A network call inside BEGIN holds locks while it waits: do it before or after."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Several writes must succeed together? Use a transaction, scoped as small as possible. Cross-service work needs ==sagas==, not one transaction."
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "All or nothing"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "ACID",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. ACID: the contract"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** four guarantees of a ==transactional== database: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability.\n**Why it exists:** a predictable contract, so app code need not patch partial failures and races by hand.\n**Used for:** judging if {icon:postgresql} PostgreSQL or {icon:mysql} MySQL InnoDB fits money and orders, versus a looser NoSQL store.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Letter",
            "Guarantee",
            "Failure it prevents"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**A**tomicity",
              "all or nothing, via undo info",
              "half-applied transfer"
            ],
            [
              "**C**onsistency",
              "constraints hold after commit",
              "negative balance"
            ],
            [
              "**I**solation",
              "behaves like a serial order",
              "race between two txns"
            ],
            [
              "**D**urability",
              "WAL fsynced before COMMIT returns",
              "lost commit on crash"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Example: transfer $100",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE TABLE accounts (",
            "  id int PRIMARY KEY,",
            "  bal int CHECK (bal >= 0));",
            "BEGIN;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal-100",
            "  WHERE id=1; -- bal was 60",
            "COMMIT; -- CHECK fails: ROLLBACK"
          ],
          "note": "Overdraft rejected, nothing applied"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Atomicity: both UPDATEs apply, or neither, using undo",
            "Consistency: CHECK and foreign keys allow only valid states",
            "Isolation: concurrent txns act as if run in some serial order",
            "Durability: after COMMIT returns, WAL is fsynced, so a crash keeps it"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Benefits vs Things to consider",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Correct money-like data",
            "No custom recovery code",
            "Predictable contract for app code"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`C` is app invariants; DB enforces only declared constraints",
            "`C` in ACID is not `C` in CAP",
            "Durability costs one fsync per commit",
            "`synchronous_commit=off` risks a few hundred ms of commits"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setting",
            "Speed",
            "Crash risk"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "synchronous_commit=on",
              "slower",
              "none"
            ],
            [
              "synchronous_commit=off",
              "faster",
              "last ~100s ms lost"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Name one failure each letter prevents: torn transfer, overdraft, race, lost commit.\nContrast with BASE systems, which trade ACID for availability and scale."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Four guarantees"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "4. Isolation Levels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** a setting for how much concurrent ==transactions== see of each other's work",
            "**Why it exists:** serial execution is correct but slow, so levels trade safety for throughput",
            "**Used for:** picking the __weakest__ level that blocks the anomalies you cannot tolerate"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS: ANOMALIES",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Dirty read:** you see another txn's uncommitted data",
            "**Non-repeatable read:** the same row read twice differs",
            "**Phantom read:** the same query returns new rows",
            "**Write skew:** two txns read, write disjoint rows, break a shared rule"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "EXAMPLE: ON-CALL DOCTORS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- T1 and T2, Repeatable Read",
            "SELECT count(*) FROM oncall; -- 2",
            "DELETE FROM oncall",
            "  WHERE doc = 'alice'; -- T1",
            "-- T2 deletes doc = 'bob'"
          ],
          "note": "Serializable aborts one with 40001"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Both see count 2, both delete, both commit: **zero** doctors remain (==write skew==). Under Serializable, one txn is aborted.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "COMPARISON TABLE",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Blocks",
            "Default in"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Read Uncommitted",
              "nothing",
              "none"
            ],
            [
              "Read Committed",
              "dirty reads",
              "PostgreSQL, Oracle"
            ],
            [
              "Repeatable Read",
              "+ non-repeatable, phantom*",
              "MySQL InnoDB"
            ],
            [
              "Serializable",
              "all, incl. write skew",
              "none"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "*Phantoms are blocked at Repeatable Read in PostgreSQL and InnoDB only."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "BENEFITS",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Higher levels give stronger correctness",
            "No application-side locking code needed",
            "Weakest sufficient level keeps throughput high"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "THINGS TO CONSIDER",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Serializable can abort: PostgreSQL error `40001`, so **retry**",
            "Assuming \"Repeatable Read\" means the same in every database",
            "Guarantees differ by __engine__, so check the docs"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Name the anomaly you are guarding against, then pick the level that blocks it.\nFor Serializable, mention retry logic for aborted transactions."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Safety vs throughput"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "5. Locking",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** a transaction reserves a row, page or table",
            "**Why it exists:** two writers on one row means one waits or fails",
            "**Used for:** stopping ==lost updates==, serializing stock decrement and seat booking"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Lock",
            "Readers",
            "Writers"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Shared (S)",
              "many at once",
              "blocked"
            ],
            [
              "Exclusive (X)",
              "blocked",
              "**one** only"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Pessimistic",
                "lines": [],
                "text": "Buyer 2 blocks at FOR UPDATE until buyer 1 commits",
                "code": [
                  "BEGIN;",
                  "SELECT stock FROM items",
                  "WHERE id=7 FOR UPDATE;",
                  "UPDATE items",
                  "SET stock=stock-1 WHERE id=7;",
                  "COMMIT;"
                ],
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "title": "Optimistic",
                "lines": [],
                "text": "0 rows updated means someone else won, so retry",
                "code": [
                  "UPDATE items",
                  "SET stock=stock-1,",
                  "version=version+1",
                  "WHERE id=7",
                  "AND version=12;"
                ],
                "ink": "blue"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Style",
            "Locks held",
            "Best when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Pessimistic",
              "yes, until COMMIT",
              "conflicts common"
            ],
            [
              "Optimistic",
              "none",
              "conflicts rare"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "THE LOST UPDATE",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "T1",
            "T2",
            "stock"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "reads 5",
              "reads 5",
              "5"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "writes 4",
              "-",
              "4"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "-",
              "writes 4",
              "4 (want 3)"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Benefits",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pessimistic is simple and safe on ==hot rows==",
            "Optimistic holds no locks, so it scales when conflicts are rare"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Things to consider",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Deadlock**: T1 locks A wants B, T2 locks B wants A; DB kills one victim",
            "Fix: always lock rows in one consistent order",
            "A table lock, or FOR UPDATE with no index, throttles throughput"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "AVOIDING DEADLOCK",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "T1 holds A",
                "sub": "wants B",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "T2 holds B",
                "sub": "wants A",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "DB kills one",
                "sub": "victim rolls back",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT * FROM accounts",
            "WHERE id IN (1, 2)",
            "ORDER BY id",
            "FOR UPDATE;"
          ],
          "note": "Same lock order in every txn"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "Index the WHERE column so FOR UPDATE locks only the matching rows."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Optimistic retry",
          "text": "0 rows updated: re-read the row, reapply your change, try again."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Pessimistic for high contention; optimistic with a version column for low contention. Always mention deadlock avoidance."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Pessimistic vs optimistic"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "6. MVCC",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** ==MVCC== keeps several versions of a row; each transaction reads a consistent snapshot",
            "**Why it exists:** pure locking makes readers and writers block each other, hurting throughput",
            "**Used for:** PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB and Oracle snapshot reads with no read locks"
          ],
          "style": "star",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every row version stores a creator and a deleter txid",
            "PostgreSQL names them `xmin` (creator) and `xmax` (deleter)",
            "A query sees only versions ==committed before its snapshot==",
            "An UPDATE writes a new version; it never edits in place"
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "Before UPDATE",
                "headers": [
                  "id",
                  "price",
                  "xmin",
                  "xmax"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "1",
                    "10",
                    "50",
                    "-"
                  ]
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "After T1 (txid 100)",
                "headers": [
                  "id",
                  "price",
                  "xmin",
                  "xmax"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "1",
                    "10",
                    "50",
                    "100"
                  ],
                  [
                    "1",
                    "20",
                    "100",
                    "-"
                  ]
                ],
                "caption": "two live versions of one row"
              }
            ],
            "arrow": true
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- T1 (txid 100)",
            "UPDATE items SET price = 20",
            "  WHERE id = 1;",
            "-- T2 (snapshot before 100)",
            "SELECT price FROM items",
            "  WHERE id = 1; -- 10"
          ],
          "note": "T2 never waits for T1"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Reader",
            "Snapshot",
            "Version",
            "Reads"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "T2 (open)",
              "before 100",
              "xmin 50",
              "10"
            ],
            [
              "T3 (new)",
              "after 100",
              "xmin 100",
              "20"
            ],
            [
              "T1 itself",
              "own write",
              "xmin 100",
              "20"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Approach",
            "Reader vs writer",
            "Same-row writers"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Pure locking",
              "block each other",
              "wait"
            ],
            [
              "{green:MVCC}",
              "**never** block",
              "still wait"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "BENEFITS",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Readers never block writers; writers never block readers",
            "Point-in-time reads: a long report sees one stable snapshot"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "THINGS TO CONSIDER",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Old versions pile up as ==dead tuples==; PostgreSQL needs `VACUUM` or tables and indexes bloat",
            "A long transaction pins old versions and blocks cleanup; watch idle-in-transaction sessions",
            "Writers on the same row still conflict: less locking, not none"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "Alert on idle-in-transaction sessions: they pin old versions and stall VACUUM."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "MVCC gives Read Committed and Repeatable Read their non-blocking reads. The cost is version storage and vacuum."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Multi-Version Concurrency"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Connection Pooling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Connection Pooling",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** a fixed set of ==open connections== that requests borrow and return",
            "**Why it exists:** each new connection costs a TCP + auth handshake (tens of ms)",
            "PostgreSQL also forks a process per connection, ~5-10 MB each",
            "**Used for:** web apps, APIs and serverless functions at high request rates"
          ],
          "style": "star",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "200 requests",
                "sub": "concurrent"
              },
              {
                "text": "Pool of 20",
                "sub": "queue for a free one",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "PostgreSQL",
                "sub": "~5 ms per query",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "What happens"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1. Borrow",
              "request takes a free connection"
            ],
            [
              "2. Query",
              "runs ~5 ms on it"
            ],
            [
              "3. Return",
              "connection goes back to pool"
            ],
            [
              "4. Wait",
              "others queue until one frees"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Tools: {icon:openjdk} **HikariCP** runs inside the app; {icon:postgresql} **PgBouncer** is an external proxy."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Pool",
            "Lives in",
            "Shared by"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "HikariCP",
              "each app instance",
              "one app"
            ],
            [
              "PgBouncer",
              "separate proxy",
              "all instances"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "SIZING RULE OF THUMB",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "pool = (cores x 2) + spindles",
            "4 cores, 1 disk:",
            "(4 x 2) + 1 = 9  ->  ~10",
            "not 500 connections"
          ],
          "note": "Size to the DB, not the traffic"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Pool size",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Too large",
              "context-switching, slower queries"
            ],
            [
              "Just right",
              "~10 busy, rest queue briefly"
            ],
            [
              "Too small",
              "requests queue and time out"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Removes per-request ==handshake== latency",
            "Caps concurrent queries below `max_connections`",
            "PgBouncer **transaction mode**: thousands of clients share a few dozen server connections"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Leaked connection (never returned after an exception) drains the pool",
            "Always release in **finally**",
            "`SET` and prepared statements break in transaction pooling"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "10 app instances x pool of 20 = 200 server connections",
            "Per-instance pools add up: do the multiplication"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "Serverless: every function opens its own connection, so put PgBouncer in front."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Bigger is not better: size the pool to the DB. Put PgBouncer in front when many app instances or serverless functions connect."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Borrow, don't open"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "8. Partitioning",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "One big table, cut into ==partitions== on a single server. Queries still see __one logical table__.",
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** one large table split into smaller physical pieces inside one DB server",
            "**Why it exists:** huge tables make scans, index upkeep and old-data deletes slow",
            "**Used for:** time-series and log data, cheap data expiry, queries filtering on the partition key"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE TABLE events (",
            "  id bigint, ts date, msg text",
            ") PARTITION BY RANGE (ts);",
            "CREATE TABLE events_2026_09",
            "  PARTITION OF events FOR VALUES",
            "  FROM ('2026-09-01') TO ('2026-10-01');"
          ],
          "note": "Key chosen up front"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "events",
                "sub": "logical table"
              },
              {
                "text": "WHERE ts >= '2026-09-15'",
                "sub": "query"
              },
              {
                "text": "events_2026_09",
                "sub": "only partition read",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Skipping every other partition is called ==partition pruning==: the planner reads only matching pieces.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. STRATEGIES",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Strategy",
            "Key example",
            "Best for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Range",
              "ts by month",
              "Dates, retention"
            ],
            [
              "List",
              "country = 'IN'",
              "Known categories"
            ],
            [
              "Hash",
              "user_id mod N",
              "Even spread"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. BENEFITS vs CONSIDER",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pruning shrinks the data scanned",
            "Each partition has smaller indexes",
            "Retention is instant `DROP PARTITION`, no slow `DELETE` plus vacuum"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No filter on the key scans **every** partition",
            "Key must be picked up front",
            "Primary and unique keys usually must include the key"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "Partitioning",
            "Sharding"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Servers",
              "One",
              "Many"
            ],
            [
              "Splits",
              "Tables on a node",
              "Data across nodes"
            ],
            [
              "Query view",
              "One table",
              "Router needed"
            ],
            [
              "Try it",
              "Earlier",
              "Last resort"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "Run EXPLAIN on your query: the plan should list only the partitions you expect."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Partition for manageability and pruning on ONE machine. Pick a key matching your most common filter."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Split big tables"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "9. Replication",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** copying a primary's data to replicas, usually by streaming the ==WAL==",
            "**Why it exists:** survive node failure (HA) and scale reads past one machine",
            "**Used for:** read replicas (dashboards, search), failover standbys, cross-region copies"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "Single-leader replication",
            "rows": [
              [
                "app"
              ],
              [
                "primary"
              ],
              [
                "r1",
                "r2"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "app": {
                "text": "App",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "laptop"
              },
              "primary": {
                "text": "Primary",
