    {
      "page_title": "10. Sharding",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** ==horizontally splitting== data across independent database servers (shards), each holding a subset of the rows.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Why it exists:** one machine's disk, memory and write throughput are finite, and __replication cannot scale writes__.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Used for:** very large datasets and write-heavy systems: social feeds, messaging, multi-tenant SaaS.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "shard_id = hash(user_id) % 4",
            "-- user 1001: hash = 7",
            "-- 7 % 4 = 3",
            "-- rows live on shard 3",
            "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id=1001;"
          ],
          "note": "router sends the query to shard 3"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "App",
                "sub": "user 1001",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Router",
                "sub": "hash 7 % 4 = 3",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "Shard 3",
                "sub": "holds the rows",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Strategy",
            "Good for",
            "Watch out"
          ],
          "rows": [
